# Closure and test-first

### Two lists that cannot drift apart

Week 6, rung 4 of 7 (Proven), talk 2

---

## One sentence, three places

```
REQUIREMENTS.md
| R-010 | Event-driven | When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page ... | lite |

tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature
  @R-010 @ears-event-driven
  Rule: When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page ...
    Scenario: The middle step links both ways

tests/bdd/steps/site/static-site.steps.ts
  Rule("When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page ...", ({ RuleScenario }) => {
```

The `Rule:` title is the requirement, word for word. The scenarios are its proof. The steps bind it.

---

## Closure: every arrow must hold

```
REQUIREMENTS.md       every id ─────────► at least one Rule with a scenario
tests/bdd/features    every Rule ───────► exactly one known id, its sentence word for word, its pattern's tag
tests/bdd/steps       every feature ────► loaded by a steps file
any test title        "R-008: ..." ─────► an id that exists
```

`pnpm test:ears:audit` (and the gate, through `tests/suite-health/requirements.test.ts`) fails when any
arrow breaks. Two lists that must agree, checked so they cannot drift: a **Closure Check**.

---

## What a broken arrow says

```
rule-title-drift: tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature:38: the Rule's title is not R-010's
sentence. One sentence, one source: copy it from REQUIREMENTS.md:21 word for word.
    REQUIREMENTS.md: When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link each step page ...
    feature:         When a lab has more than one step, the site shall link every step page ...

ears-tag: tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature:38: R-010 is Event-driven, so its Rule takes
exactly @ears-event-driven; it has @ears-ubiquitous.

unknown-id: tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature:38: @R-099 is not in REQUIREMENTS.md. State the
requirement there first, with --next-id, then prove it here.

unknown-citation: tests/unit/lite/html.test.ts: cites R-077, which is not in REQUIREMENTS.md.
```

Every one names the file, the line and the fix: L4.

---

## The audit and the runner guard different things

Change one word of R-010's `Rule:` title:

| Check | Fails with | Guards |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pnpm test:ears:audit` | `rule-title-drift` | the **sentence**: one source, word for word |
| `pnpm test:bdd` (vitest-cucumber) | `FeatureUknowRuleError: Rule: ... does not exist` | the **binding**: feature and steps agree |

Neither alone is enough. The audit cannot run a scenario; the runner cannot read `REQUIREMENTS.md`.

---

## Inside the audit

```ts
// variants/rung-4-proven/scripts/checks/ears-audit.ts
export const PATTERNS: Record<string, { tag: string; opening: RegExp; form: string }> = {
  Ubiquitous: { tag: "@ears-ubiquitous", opening: /^The\b/, form: "The <system> shall <response>." },
  "Event-driven": { tag: "@ears-event-driven", opening: /^When\b[^,]*,/, form: "When <trigger>, ..." },
  "State-driven": { tag: "@ears-state-driven", opening: /^While\b[^,]*,/, ... },
  Optional: { tag: "@ears-optional", opening: /^Where\b[^,]*,/, ... },
  "Unwanted behaviour": { tag: "@ears-unwanted", opening: /^If\b[^]*,\s*then\b/, ... },
};
export const SYSTEMS: Record<string, string> = { "the generator": "gen", "the site": "lite", "the command": "cli" };
```

Pure functions: `parseRequirements`, `auditRequirements`, `auditFeatures`, `auditCitations`, `nextId`.
`tests/suite-health/requirements.test.ts` runs each on negative fixtures, then on the real files.

---

## Ids are identities

- Take the next one: `pnpm test:ears:audit --next-id` prints `R-013`.
- It is one past the highest ever used, **retired ones included**.
- Retired ids live in `tests/bdd/retired-requirement-ids.txt` and are never reused (`retired-id`).
- Changing a requirement's wording keeps its id. A different requirement gets a new id.

Upstream: four-digit `@rule-NNNN` tags, one counter for the repository, `tests/bdd/ears-retired-rule-ids.txt`.
`--next-id` printed `0786` at `2b96b69`. A release claim cites a rule by id, so an id must never move.

---

## Test-first, enforced

Add a requirement and nothing else:

```
| R-013 | Ubiquitous | The command shall write tutors.json beside the pages it writes. | cli |
```

```
unproved-requirement: REQUIREMENTS.md:24 R-013: no Rule tagged @R-013 has a scenario. Add one to the
feature for its layer in tests/bdd/features.
 ELIFECYCLE  Command failed with exit code 1.
```

A requirement without proof is a wish, and the gate refuses wishes. A scenario without a requirement cites
nothing (`unknown-id`). Either way, the sentence comes first.

---

## Standard work: "Behaviour starts as a sentence"

```markdown
<!-- variants/rung-4-proven/AGENTS.md -->
1. Take an id: `pnpm test:ears:audit --next-id`.
2. Add the EARS sentence to `REQUIREMENTS.md`, with its pattern and layer.
3. Copy it, word for word, as a `Rule:` ... with a scenario. Bind the steps. See it fail.
4. Implement it in the layer the row names. See it pass.
5. Add a unit or property test for the new code, so the mutation run can see it ... Hold every floor.
```

And under "Ask a human first": "Changing or retiring a requirement in `REQUIREMENTS.md`."

---

## Upstream's standard work

`.claude/skills/ears-gherkin-dev/SKILL.md` walks an agent through the same loop: write the Rule, show it to
the human and **wait for a yes**, prove it red, implement, prove it green, audit. It also says: "Do not add
cucumber-js or a second runner."

`guides/testing/EARS-METHODOLOGY.md` is the long form: patterns, system names, ids, audit codes, the
`@active`/`@inactive` rule and the release harness that cites rules by id.

```
deno run -A scripts/checks/ears-audit.ts
OK: 85 feature file(s), 441 Rule(s), 0 new violation(s), 0 baselined, 0 stale baseline entries.
```

---

## Lean: standard work and the waste it removes

| Without it | Waste | Removed by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| requirements in a doc, tests in a folder, drifting | defects, relearning | the closure audit |
| behaviour added because an agent thought it useful | extra features (overproduction) | no id, no Rule, no merge |
| a sentence argued about after the code exists | rework | a person approves the sentence first |
| "what does R-010 mean?" | relearning | one sentence, one source, word for word |

Standard work is not a cage. It is the baseline you improve: in the lab you will propose one change to it.

---

## Which levels guard the rules

| Rule | Level |
| --- | --- |
| every requirement is proved; every citation is known | L4, the closure audit in the gate |
| a Rule's title is its sentence, word for word | L4, `rule-title-drift` |
| each sentence has EARS form, a system, a layer | L4, `shall-count`, `ears-form`, ... |
| a scenario's steps match its feature | L3, vitest-cucumber's own errors |
| the pattern fits the meaning | L2 and a person: `AGENTS.md`, "Ask a human first" |
| behaviour starts as a sentence a person approves | Human Boundary |

---

## Take away

- One sentence, one id, one source; repeat it, never paraphrase it
- The closure audit keeps requirements and proof together, in the gate
- Ids are identities: never reused
- Test-first is enforced: no proof, no green
- Standard work is the baseline for improvement, not the end of it
